---
import PreciseFooter from '../components/PreciseFooter.astro';
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>AI World Simulator in the Browser — Aliveville</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Play a real AI world simulator free in your browser. Aliveville runs autonomous NPCs with memory, relationships, and grudges in a persistent 3D town — no install."
    />
    <meta
      name="keywords"
      content="AI world simulator, browser AI game, autonomous NPCs, 3D AI town, playable AI simulation, persistent AI agents, AI simulation game"
    />
    <meta name="author" content="Sarthak Agrawal" />
    <meta
      name="robots"
      content="index, follow, max-image-preview:large, max-snippet:-1, max-video-preview:-1"
    />
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://aliveville.com/ai-world-simulator" />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://aliveville.com/ai-world-simulator" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="AI World Simulator in the Browser — Aliveville" />
    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="What an AI world simulator is, how Aliveville runs autonomous NPCs with memory in a 3D town, and and the current status: a paused experiment with the hosted game offline."
    />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Aliveville" />
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://aliveville.com/assets/ashment_intro_square.jpg" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://aliveville.com/assets/ashment_intro_square.jpg" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="any" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />
    <script type="application/ld+json">
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What is an AI world simulator?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "An AI world simulator is a persistent environment where AI-controlled characters act on their own goals instead of following a fixed script. In Aliveville that means a small 3D town of agents who remember people, hold grudges, form relationships, and keep living while you watch or wander."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Can I play Aliveville right now?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Not currently. Aliveville is a paused experiment and the hosted game is offline. The source is open on GitHub."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Do I need to install anything?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Nothing to install for the web client, but the hosted game is offline (paused experiment), so it must be run locally from the GitHub source."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Do the AI characters remember what happens?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Yes. Agents keep memory and social state across encounters — who they met, what they were offered, and how prior interactions went — so later conversations carry consequences."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "How is this different from AI Dungeon or a chatbot?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Chatbots and text adventures generate a response when you prompt them. Aliveville's agents keep running their day when you look away: the simulation loop drives goals, movement, memory, and conflict whether or not you are typing."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    </script>
  </head>
  <body style="margin:0;background:#010102;">
    <main
      style="max-width:42rem;margin:0 auto;padding:4rem 1.5rem;line-height:1.7;color:#d1d1d6;font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif;"
    >
      <a href="/" style="font-size:0.75rem;color:#888;">← Aliveville</a>
      <h1 style="margin-top:1rem;font-size:2rem;color:#fff;">
        AI world simulator: autonomous civilization agents (paused experiment)
      </h1>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">
        An <strong>AI world simulator</strong> is a persistent environment where characters act on their
        own goals instead of waiting for a scripted quest marker. Aliveville is a browser-playable 3D
        version of that idea: a small town of autonomous agents who remember people, hold grudges, form
        relationships, and create emergent stories while you walk around.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Status: paused experiment.</strong> The hosted game was shut down on 2026-09-26. See how it
        works below, or <a href="https://github.com/Significant-Hobbies/aliveville" style="color:#f8d44e">read the source on GitHub</a>.
      </p>

      <img
        src="/assets/ashment_intro_square.jpg"
        alt="Aliveville's 3D town — an AI world simulator where autonomous characters remember and react"
        style="width:100%;border-radius:0.75rem;margin:1.5rem 0 0;display:block;"
        loading="lazy"
      />

      <h2 style="color:#fff;font-size:1.25rem;margin:2rem 0 0.75rem">How Aliveville differs from chat NPCs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Agents keep living when you look away</strong> — the day continues; they are not only
          dialogue trees.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Memory and social state</strong> — who they met, what they were offered, and how prior
          encounters went.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>3D town you can explore</strong> — walk the map; overhear and interrupt instead of only
          opening a chat panel.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Browser-native</strong> — try the public build without downloading a client.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="color:#fff;font-size:1.25rem;margin:2rem 0 0.75rem">What a session looks like</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Open the game and drop into the town — agents are already mid-routine.</li>
        <li>Walk up to a character and talk. They answer with memory of who you are and what happened before.</li>
        <li>Watch the consequences spread: an argument, a favor, a rumor — the town state changes even when the plot is not yours.</li>
        <li>Come back later: relationships, grudges, and quests persist across visits.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 style="color:#fff;font-size:1.25rem;margin:2rem 0 0.75rem">How Aliveville compares</h2>
      <p>
        “AI world simulator” covers a few different products. An honest map of the space:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Aliveville</strong> — a 3D town of autonomous agents with memory, playable free in the
          browser. Small, persistent, and open-source.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>World Simulator AI (worldsimulator.ai)</strong> — describe a world in text and it
          generates a playable scenario around it. Generative breadth rather than one persistent town.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AI Dungeon and text adventures</strong> — a narrator that responds to your prompts.
          Flexible storytelling, but the world only moves when you type.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Research platforms (SimWorld and friends)</strong> — simulators built to train and
          evaluate agents at scale. Powerful, but aimed at researchers with an engine install, not at
          players.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="color:#fff;font-size:1.25rem;margin:2rem 0 0.75rem">Who this is for</h2>
      <p>
        Players who want an AI civilization / life-sim sandbox; builders studying agent memory and
        multi-agent social loops; anyone comparing “AI world simulator” products that claim autonomous
        NPCs rather than single-player chat skins.
      </p>

      <h2 style="color:#fff;font-size:1.25rem;margin:2rem 0 0.75rem">Frequently asked</h2>
      <h3 style="color:#e8e8ec;font-size:1rem;margin:1.25rem 0 0.25rem">Can I play Aliveville right now?</h3>
      <p style="margin-top:0;">
        No. The hosted game is offline; the <a href="https://github.com/Significant-Hobbies/aliveville" style="color:#f8d44e">source is on GitHub</a>.
      </p>
      <h3 style="color:#e8e8ec;font-size:1rem;margin:1.25rem 0 0.25rem">Do I need to install anything?</h3>
      <p style="margin-top:0;">The hosted game is offline; run it locally from the GitHub source.</p>
      <h3 style="color:#e8e8ec;font-size:1rem;margin:1.25rem 0 0.25rem">Do the characters actually remember?</h3>
      <p style="margin-top:0;">
        Yes. Memory and social state persist across encounters, so conversations carry consequences
        between sessions.
      </p>
      <h3 style="color:#e8e8ec;font-size:1rem;margin:1.25rem 0 0.25rem">Is it still being built?</h3>
      <p style="margin-top:0;">
        Aliveville is an early, opinionated experiment — paused for now, bounded in scope, and open
        about what it is not yet.
      </p>

      <h2 style="color:#fff;font-size:1.25rem;margin:2rem 0 0.75rem">Try it</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Get the code from <a href="https://github.com/Significant-Hobbies/aliveville" style="color:#f8d44e">GitHub</a> (the hosted game is offline).
        </li>
        <li>Run it locally to explore the town and watch agents act without you driving every line of dialogue.</li>
        <li>
          Read the product overview on the <a href="/" style="color:#f8d44e">homepage</a> or the
          machine-readable brief in <a href="/llms.txt" style="color:#f8d44e">llms.txt</a>.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2 style="color:#fff;font-size:1.25rem;margin:2rem 0 0.75rem">Limits (honest)</h2>
      <p>
        Aliveville is an early, opinionated simulator — not a full MMORPG or a research benchmark suite.
        Agent count, world size, and dialogue quality are bounded by the public browser build. Claims
        about “living AI people” mean autonomous simulation loops with memory, not general intelligence.
      </p>

